  "outcome": "O1",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O1",
    "direction": "mixed",
    "magnitude": "moderate",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "Requiring landlords to upgrade to EPC C by 2028 should cut energy costs for renters in inefficient homes, but could push some landlords to sell up, shrinking rental supply and pushing rents up. The net effect on affordability depends heavily on whether landlord exits happen at scale.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether the compliance cost \u2014 estimated at around \u00a39,000 per property on average \u2014 triggers large-scale landlord exits and reduces rental supply, or whether the sector absorbs it as previous regulatory changes suggest it might.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy requires landlords to upgrade properties to EPC C or above by 2028.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Reintroduce requirements for landlords to upgrade the energy efficiency of their properties to EPC C or above by 2028."
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Around 58% of private rented sector properties already meet EPC C, meaning roughly 42% do not.",
        "evidence_id": "E32",
        "quote": "around 58% of private rented sector properties already meet the EPC C standard"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Over 70% of low-income private renters live in homes with poor energy efficiency.",
        "evidence_id": "E28",
        "quote": "over 70% of low-income private renters live in homes with poor energy efficiency"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "A home rated EPC E costs on average \u00a3595 more per year to heat than one rated EPC C; F/G-rated homes cost \u00a31,339 more per year.",
        "evidence_id": "E14",
        "quote": "a home with an EPC band E costs, on average, \u00a3595 more per year to heat than one rated EPC C, and a home in band F/G costs \u00a31,339 per year more"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Tenants are expected to benefit from lower energy bills and warmer, healthier homes once upgrades are complete.",
        "evidence_id": "E12",
        "quote": "Tenants are expected to benefit from lower energy bills and improved living conditions, including warmer homes less susceptible to damp and mould"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "There are concerns that landlords with older, harder-to-treat properties may sell up rather than invest, reducing rental supply and increasing rents.",
        "evidence_id": "E16",
        "quote": "some landlords, particularly those with older, harder-to-treat properties, may choose to sell up rather than invest in costly upgrades, potentially reducing the supply of available rental properties and increasing rents"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Landlord groups argue that insufficient financial support makes compliance unrealistic for smaller landlords.",
        "evidence_id": "E38",
        "quote": "the government needs to provide clearer and more substantial financial assistance, such as low-interest loans or grants, to make compliance realistic and affordable for smaller landlords"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "This policy creates a genuine tension between two housing affordability pressures. On the benefit side: over 70% of low-income private renters live in energy-inefficient homes, and those in EPC E-rated properties pay roughly \u00a3595 more per year on heating than they would in a C-rated home. Mandatory upgrades \u2014 if delivered \u2014 would directly cut these costs for the renters who most need relief. That is a real affordability gain concentrated on lower-income tenants. On the risk side: roughly 42% of private rented properties still need upgrading, and the typical upgrade cost (~\u00a39,000) is steep \u2014 around 75% of a single-property landlord's median annual rental income. For landlords with older, solid-wall properties, costs are higher still. At these levels, a meaningful share of smaller landlords may exit rather than comply, as landlord bodies warn. Supply contraction would push rents up, potentially hurting the very tenants the policy aims to help. The Resolution Foundation argues large-scale exit is unlikely, citing sector stability through previous regulatory shocks. But this remains a contested projection. The net verdict is mixed: the energy-cost savings for sitting tenants are real and evidence-backed; the supply-side risk is credible but contested. The policy improves quality-of-home affordability while risking worsening supply-side affordability, with the balance depending chiefly on the scale of landlord exit \u2014 a parameter the evidence cannot resolve with confidence.",
